p2u: 1
deck:
  title: Typst First-Class
  subtitle: Typesetting math and code with Typst
  theme: writebook
  meta:
    engine: typst
slides:
  - layout: title
    title: Typst First-Class
    subtitle: Typesetting math, code and layout with Typst
    notes: |
      Typst is a modern typesetting system. In Present2u it is a first-class
      render engine next to D2 and LaTeX: any ```typst fence is compiled to an
      SVG and embedded in the slide.

  - layout: content
    title: What Is Typst?
    body: |
      Typst is a typesetting system with a friendly syntax. Inline math uses the
      same dollar delimiters as LaTeX, so $E = m c^2$ and $q = \lfloor N/2
      \rfloor + 1$ render with KaTeX right in the slide.

      - Markup, math and code live in one document
      - Compiles in milliseconds
      - Great for equations, tables and small figures

  - layout: diagram
    title: Typst Equation
    blocks:
      - kind: diagram
        lang: typst
        source: |
          #set page(width: auto, height: auto, margin: 10pt)
          #set text(size: 18pt)
          $ q = floor(N/2) + 1 $
    notes: A Typst snippet compiled to SVG and inlined into the slide.

  - layout: diagram
    title: Typst Document
    blocks:
      - kind: diagram
        lang: typst
        source: |
          #set page(width: auto, height: auto, margin: 12pt)
          #set text(size: 15pt)
          = Raft
          #align(center)[_Consensus, made understandable_]

          A *leader* is elected by a majority.

          $ "term" = "term" + 1 $
    notes: Typst can lay out headings, emphasis and math together.

  - layout: content
    title: Fenced Typst in Markdown
    body: |
      Drop a Typst block straight into a slide:

      ```typst
      #set page(width: auto, height: auto, margin: 10pt)
      $ integral_0^1 x^2 dif x = 1/3 $
      ```

      Code fences still get syntax highlighting:

      ```python
      def quorum(n):
          return n // 2 + 1
      ```

  - layout: recap
    title: Recap
    body: |
      - ```typst``` fences compile to SVG server-side
      - Inline math renders with KaTeX via markdown-it
      - Typst, D2 and LaTeX all live side by side
      - The exported deck is still a single self-contained HTML file